.TH P3M-SYNC 1 "@DATE@" "p3m @VERSION@" "p3m Manual"
.SH NAME
p3m-sync \- parallel one-way directory synchronisation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p3m-sync
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I SOURCE
.I DEST
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B p3m-sync
makes the directory
.I DEST
an exact mirror of the directory
.I SOURCE
by walking both trees in lockstep with a pool of worker threads.
.I DEST
is created if it does not exist.
The
.I contents
of
.I SOURCE
(not
.I SOURCE
itself) land directly in
.IR DEST .
.PP
By default this is a
.B dry run
that lists, as CSV, everything that would change; add
.B \-\-apply
to synchronise.
.PP
A regular file is
.B updated
when its size or modification time differs.
Modification times are preserved on every copy, so a synchronised tree
compares equal on the next run.
Mode (and, with
.BR \-\-owner ,
owner and group) are kept in step; when only those differ the entry is
reported as
.B metadata
and no data is copied.
.PP
File data, symbolic links and special files are written to a temporary name
in the destination directory and renamed into place, so a destination file is
never observed half-written and a failed write leaves the previous version
untouched.
Directory modes and times are applied after the walk, deepest first.
.PP
Entries that exist only at
.I DEST
are left alone unless
.B \-\-delete
is given.
Symbolic links are recreated as links and never followed.
Hard links are not preserved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Actually synchronise (the default is a dry run).
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Remove entries that exist at
.I DEST
but not at
.IR SOURCE ,
children before parents.
Also allows an entry whose type differs between the two sides (a file where
the other has a directory, for example) to be replaced; without
.B \-\-delete
such conflicts are skipped and reported.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-checksum
For files of equal size, compare the contents byte for byte instead of
trusting the modification time.
Files with identical contents but different times are reported as
.BR metadata ,
not
.BR update .
.TP
.B \-\-owner
Also synchronise owner and group, where permitted.
.TP
.BI \-\-modify\-window " SECONDS"
Treat modification times within
.I SECONDS
of each other as equal.
Use this with file systems that store coarse timestamps.
The default is 0 (nanosecond-exact).
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-threads " \fIN\fR"
Worker threads (default: the number of online CPUs).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the CSV to
.I FILE
and show a live progress display.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the console listing; progress and the summary are still shown, and a
.B \-o
file is still written.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
CSV with the columns
.IR path ,
.IR type ,
.IR size ,
.IR action ,
.IR result .
.I path
is relative to the roots (\fB.\fR for the root itself).
Only entries that change are listed; the summary counts the rest as unchanged.
.I action
is one of
.BR create ,
.BR update ,
.B metadata
or
.BR delete .
.I result
is
.B pending
in a dry run and
.B done
once applied, or
.B failed: \fIreason\fR
or
.B skipped: \fIreason\fR.
.PP
Deleted directories are listed after their contents.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
The file system root can never be the destination.
.IP \(bu 2
.I SOURCE
and
.I DEST
may not be the same directory or contain one another.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-\-delete ,
an empty
.I SOURCE
is refused when
.I DEST
is not empty, so a source that failed to mount cannot empty the destination.
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is deleted from a directory whose source listing could not be read
completely.
.IP \(bu 2
Symbolic links are never followed, and every directory is opened with
.BR O_NOFOLLOW .
.SH SIGNALS
On the first
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGTERM
during
.BR \-\-apply ,
.B p3m-sync
prints a notice, stops picking up new directories and lets all in-flight work
finish; because updates are renamed into place, no destination file is left
truncated.
The summary reports
.B interrupted
and the exit status is 130 (SIGINT) or 143 (SIGTERM).
Re-running the same command completes the job.
A second signal terminates immediately.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Completed with errors.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, invalid source or destination, or a safety refusal.
.TP
.BR 130 ", " 143
Interrupted by SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would change:
.PP
.RS
.nf
p3m\-sync /data/project /backup/project
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mirror it, removing files that no longer exist at the source:
.PP
.RS
.nf
p3m\-sync \-\-apply \-\-delete \-j 16 /data/project /mnt/nfs/project
.fi
.RE
.PP
Verify contents rather than trust timestamps, saving the change list:
.PP
.RS
.nf
p3m\-sync \-c \-q \-o changes.csv /data/project /backup/project
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR p3m (7),
.BR p3m\-cp (1),
.BR p3m\-diff (1),
.BR p3m\-rm (1),
.BR rsync (1)
